,oy","np":"oy|oy|oy|oy"},{"word":"אויך","gloss":"also","component":"","group":"5","klal":"","ney":"eykh","cy":"o:kh, oukh","sey":"oykh, e(y)kh","hint":"","notes":"","nw":"אויך","ng":"also","np":"|eykh|o:kh,oukh|oykh,eykh"},{"word":"אויף","gloss":"on (preposition)","component":"","group":"5","klal":"af, oyf*","ney":"af","cy":"of","sey":"of","hint":"","notes":"*oyf is not the prescribed form but has long been an interdialectal/literary form, widely used in Yiddish schools and among Yiddishists since at least the mid 20th century.","nw":"אויף","ng":"onpreposition","np":"af,oyf*|af|of|of"},{"word":"אויפֿ-","gloss":"up- (prefix)","component":"","group":"5","klal":"uf, oyf*","ney":"uf","cy":"of, ouf","sey":"if, of","hint":"","notes":"Also אַרויף. *See note at אויף. Source shows '(קאָנווערט)' beside this entry; meaning to confirm.","nw":"אויפ","ng":"upprefix","np":"uf,oyf*|uf|of,ouf|if,of"},{"word":"אַרויף","gloss":"up","component":"","group":"5","klal":"uf, oyf*","ney":"uf","cy":"of, ouf","sey":"if, of","hint":"","notes":"Pattern of אויפֿ-.","nw":"ארויף","ng":"up","np":"uf,oyf*|uf|of,ouf|if,of"},{"word":"צוזויפֿ-","gloss":"","component":"","group":"5","klal":"oyf","ney":"eyf","cy":"ouf","sey":"oyf","hint":"","notes":"","nw":"צוזויפ","ng":"","np":"oyf|eyf|ouf|oyf"},{"word":"מויד","gloss":"maiden, girl","component":"","group":"5","klal":"moyd","ney":"moyd","cy":"mɔ:d, moyd","sey":"moyd","hint":"","notes":"Also in Group 1 list; see its footnote.","nw":"מויד","ng":"maiden,girl","np":"moyd|moyd|mɔ:d,moyd|moyd"},{"word":"גוי","gloss":"non-Jew","component":"","group":"5","klal":"goy","ney":"goy","cy":"gɔ:, gou, goy","sey":"goy","hint":"","notes":"","nw":"גוי","ng":"nonjew","np":"goy|goy|gɔ:,gou,goy|goy"},{"word":"אויטאָ-","gloss":"auto-","component":"","group":"5","klal":"oyto","ney":"","cy":"","sey":"","hint":"","notes":"Colloquially oto-; in former Soviet areas, avto-. Also spelled אַוטאָ-.","nw":"אויטא","ng":"auto","np":"oyto|||"}],"rules":[{"code":"","name":"Dialect legend","when":"Klal = Standard Yiddish. NEY = Northeastern (“Litvish”). CY = Central (“Polish”). SEY = Southeastern (“Ukrainian”). SY = CY + SEY. MHG = Middle High German.","pron":"The vowel spelled וי is “oy” in Klal, but really stands for two vowels whose pronunciation differs by dialect."},{"code":"1","name":"Group 1: U4 vowel","when":"Mostly from MHG ū (Weinreich’s “U4”). Near-exhaustive list; the most important group to memorize. Umlauted forms (diminutive, plural) have ײַ rather than ײ.","pron":"Klal: oy. NEY: Lithuania oy, Belarus uy, occasionally u. SEY: oy; u in Podolia and Bessarabia (retreating to oy by the early 20th century). CY: Polish ɔ: or ɔɔ, esp. Hungarian ou."},{"code":"1m","name":"Group 1: U4 vowel + m","when":"Derived from MHG ūm.","pron":"Klal: oy. NEY: often a (not in זוימען, פֿלוים), otherwise Lithuania oy, Belarus uy. SEY: o. CY: Polish ɔ:, esp. Hungarian ou."},{"code":"1rw","name":"Group 1: U4 vowel + r/w","when":"Derived from MHG ūr/ūw.","pron":"Klal: oy. NEY: as Group 1. SEY: ov is widespread (badovern, boven, zover, etc.), otherwise as Group 1. CY: as Group 1."},{"code":"2","name":"Group 2: “cholam” vowel","when":"Virtually all other words spelled with וי, so this set need not be memorized. Mostly from MHG ou or ō, plus some Slavic borrowings. Umlauted forms have ײ rather than ײַ. Also: Semitic-component words with a stressed cholam in an open syllable (plurals דורות, סודות; singulars דור, סוד keep o universally).","pron":"Klal: oy. NEY: ey. SY: oy."},{"code":"2x","name":"Group 2 exception: closed syllable","when":"Stressed cholam in a closed syllable that nevertheless follows Group 2. Typically a younger or learned layer of the Semitic component.","pron":"Klal: oy. NEY: ey. SY: oy."},{"code":"2o","name":"Group 2 exception: open syllable with o","when":"Stressed cholam in an open syllable that is nevertheless o in all dialects. Typically the most ancient layer of the Semitic component.","pron":"o in all dialects."},{"code":"4","name":"Group 4: (near-)universal oy","when":"Borrowings whose source language has oy, or ou/au nativized as oy.","pron":"oy in all dialects. Speakers from German-influenced regions (incl. Hungary/Unterland) may say ou in German borrowings."},{"code":"5","name":"Group 5: irregular words","when":"Words with their own pattern in each dialect.","pron":"See the per-dialect columns on the word’s card."}],"pairs":{"head":["Group 1 (“U4”)","Meaning","Group 2 (“cholam”)","Meaning"],"rows":[["דאָס מויל","mouth","דער מוהל","mohel"],["די טויב","dove, pigeon","טויב","deaf"],["לויט","according to","דער לויט","measure (½ oz.)"],["די הויב","hood","געהויבן","lifted"],["די קויל","bullet, ball","די קוילן | קוילען","coals | to slaughter"],["טוישן","to (ex)change","אַנטוישן","to disappoint"],["דער הויפֿן","pile, bunch","דער הויף","courtyard"],["דאָס הויז","house","דער הויז; די הויזן","pant leg; pants"],["די לויז","louse","לויז","loose"]]}}